  ],
  "ai_usage": "Use when motion should feel physical rather than merely timed — a toast landing, a toggle knob, a badge popping in, a card snapping to a grid slot. Store the three linear() values as --spring-gentle / --spring-stiff / --spring-bouncy tokens and reference them in animation or transition, exactly like a cubic-bezier token.\n\nPick by overshoot budget: gentle (8%) for anything routine, stiff (7%, faster settle) for state toggles, bouncy (42%) only for deliberate delight on small elements. The overshoot is a MULTIPLE of the animated distance, so a bouncy 300px slide overshoots by 126px — reserve the space or it clips.\n\nGenerate your own from a damped harmonic oscillator: x(t) = 1 - e^(-d*t)*(cos(w*t) + (d/w)*sin(w*t)) sampled evenly over t in [0,1]; higher w = more oscillations, higher d = faster damping. Pin the first sample to 0 and the last to 1 so the animation starts and ends exactly on its endpoints.\n\nAvoid springing anything large or text-bearing (it induces motion sickness and hurts readability), springing a property that triggers layout (animate transform, not width/left/top), and springing an element the user is trying to click — it moves the target out from under the pointer. Always ship the reduced-motion guard.",
  "path": "effects/motion-principles/spring-physics.html",
  "source": "<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1\" />\n  <title>Spring Physics Easing</title>\n  <script type=\"application/json\" id=\"effect-meta\">\n  {\n    \"id\": \"spring-physics\",\n    \"title\": \"Spring Physics Easing\",\n    \"summary\": \"Spring curves as pure CSS linear() tokens, racing a plain ease-out so you can see the overshoot and settle.\",\n    \"description\": \"A cubic-bezier can never leave the 0–1 range on the way out, so it can ease but never overshoot. A damped spring can. Each lane here runs the same travel through a different timing function against a dashed target line: the ease-out lane stops dead on the line, while the spring lanes sail past it and settle back. The spring curves are sampled from a damped harmonic oscillator and baked into CSS linear() tokens, so they need no JavaScript and no animation library.\",\n    \"theme\": \"motion-principles\",\n    \"categories\": [\"motion\", \"easing\", \"design-tokens\", \"reference\"],\n    \"tags\": [\"spring\", \"easing\", \"linear\", \"overshoot\", \"damping\", \"motion\", \"tokens\", \"timing-function\", \"physics\"],\n    \"tech\": [\"css\"],\n    \"era\": \"2020s\",\n    \"difficulty\": \"intermediate\",\n    \"dependencies\": [],\n    \"browser_support\": \"linear() easing: Chrome 113+, Firefox 112+, Safari 17.2+. Older browsers ignore the unknown timing function and fall back to the ease declared before it, so motion still happens, just without overshoot.\",\n    \"performance_notes\": \"Four compositor-friendly loops. linear() costs no more than a cubic-bezier — it is resolved once, not sampled per frame. Animating transform rather than left keeps every lane off the layout path.\",\n    \"accessibility_notes\": \"All motion stops under prefers-reduced-motion and each dot rests on its target. Overshoot draws the eye, so keep it for small, self-contained elements (toasts, badges, toggles) and off anything a user is reading or aiming at.\",\n    \"customization\": [\n      { \"name\": \"--spring-gentle\", \"description\": \"8% overshoot, settles fast — the safe default for UI\", \"default\": \"linear(...) sampled at d=8, w=10\" },\n      { \"name\": \"--spring-stiff\", \"description\": \"7% overshoot with a quicker settle — good for toggles and tabs\", \"default\": \"linear(...) sampled at d=12, w=14\" },\n      { \"name\": \"--spring-bouncy\", \"description\": \"42% overshoot with visible secondary bounces — playful, use sparingly\", \"default\": \"linear(...) sampled at d=5, w=18\" },\n      { \"name\": \"--travel\", \"description\": \"How far the dot moves; overshoot is a multiple of this, so leave room after it\", \"default\": \"62%\" },\n      { \"name\": \"duration\", \"description\": \"Springs read as stiffer when shortened; retune damping rather than going below ~300ms\", \"default\": \"1.5s\" }\n    ],\n    \"variations\": [\n      \"Sample your own: x(t) = 1 - e^(-d·t)·(cos(w·t) + (d/w)·sin(w·t)), t from 0 to 1\",\n      \"Raise w for more oscillations, raise d to damp them out sooner\",\n      \"Apply to scale instead of translate for a pop-in\",\n      \"Pair with @starting-style for an entrance that springs on first paint\"\n    ],\n    \"related\": [\"easing-showcase\", \"duration-scale\", \"stagger-choreography\"],\n    \"ai_usage\": \"Use when motion should feel physical rather than merely timed — a toast landing, a toggle knob, a badge popping in, a card snapping to a grid slot. Store the three linear() values as --spring-gentle / --spring-stiff / --spring-bouncy tokens and reference them in animation or transition, exactly like a cubic-bezier token.\\n\\nPick by overshoot budget: gentle (8%) for anything routine, stiff (7%, faster settle) for state toggles, bouncy (42%) only for deliberate delight on small elements. The overshoot is a MULTIPLE of the animated distance, so a bouncy 300px slide overshoots by 126px — reserve the space or it clips.\\n\\nGenerate your own from a damped harmonic oscillator: x(t) = 1 - e^(-d*t)*(cos(w*t) + (d/w)*sin(w*t)) sampled evenly over t in [0,1]; higher w = more oscillations, higher d = faster damping. Pin the first sample to 0 and the last to 1 so the animation starts and ends exactly on its endpoints.\\n\\nAvoid springing anything large or text-bearing (it induces motion sickness and hurts readability), springing a property that triggers layout (animate transform, not width/left/top), and springing an element the user is trying to click — it moves the target out from under the pointer. Always ship the reduced-motion guard.\"\n  }\n  </script>\n  <style>\n    :root {\n      /* Sampled from x(t) = 1 - e^(-d·t)·(cos(w·t) + (d/w)·sin(w·t)), 40 even steps. */\n      --spring-gentle: linear(0, 0.0447, 0.1546, 0.2992, 0.4547, 0.6047, 0.7383, 0.8498, 0.9372, 1.0009, 1.0436, 1.0686, 1.0796, 1.0803, 1.074, 1.0636, 1.0513, 1.0388, 1.0271, 1.017, 1.0089, 1.0026, 0.9982, 0.9954, 0.9939, 0.9934, 0.9937, 0.9943, 0.9953, 0.9963, 0.9973, 0.9982, 0.9989, 0.9995, 1, 1.0003, 1.0004, 1.0005, 1.0005, 1.0005, 1);\n      --spring-stiff: linear(0, 0.0864, 0.2772, 0.4954, 0.6944, 0.8516, 0.9611, 1.0274, 1.0594, 1.0677, 1.0616, 1.0486, 1.0338, 1.0204, 1.0098, 1.0025, 0.9981, 0.9959, 0.9954, 0.9958, 0.9967, 0.9977, 0.9986, 0.9993, 0.9998, 1.0001, 1.0003, 1.0003, 1.0003, 1.0002, 1.0002, 1.0001, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 1);\n      --spring-bouncy: linear(0, 0.0987, 0.3464, 0.6632, 0.9737, 1.2206, 1.371, 1.4178, 1.3751, 1.2708, 1.1382, 1.0087, 0.9063, 0.8442, 0.8254, 0.8439, 0.8878, 0.9433, 0.9973, 1.0398, 1.0654, 1.0729, 1.065, 1.0465, 1.0232, 1.0008, 0.9831, 0.9725, 0.9695, 0.973, 0.9808, 0.9905, 0.9998, 1.0072, 1.0115, 1.0127, 1.0112, 1.008, 1.0039, 1, 1);\n      --ease-out: cubic-bezier(.22,1,.36,1);\n      --travel: 62%;\n      --accent: #7c5cff;\n      --accent-2: #2dd4bf;\n    }\n    * { box-sizing: border-box; }\n    html, body { margin: 0; height: 100%; }\n    body {\n      background: #0b0d12; color: #e7ebf3; font-family: system-ui, sans-serif;\n      padding: 1.3rem; display: grid; align-content: center; gap: .75rem;\n    }\n    .head { font-size: .72rem; color: #8b93a7; margin: 0 0 .25rem; }\n    .head b { color: #e7ebf3; font-weight: 600; }\n    .head i { font-style: normal; color: #5f6883; }\n    .lane { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 116px 1fr; align-items: center; gap: .8rem; }\n    .lane b { font-family: ui-monospace, Menlo, monospace; font-size: .68rem; font-weight: 500; color: #8b93a7; }\n    .track {\n      position: relative; height: 24px; border-radius: 999px;\n      background: #151925; border: 1px solid #262c3d;\n    }\n    /* The target the motion is aiming at — anything past this line is overshoot. */\n    .track::after {\n      content: \"\"; position: absolute; top: -4px; bottom: -4px;\n      left: calc(var(--travel) + 12px); width: 1px;\n      background: repeating-linear-gradient(#3d465e 0 3px, transparent 3px 6px);\n    }\n    .dot {\n      position: absolute; top: 3px; left: 0; margin-left: 3px;\n      width: 18px; height: 18px; border-radius: 50%;\n      background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--accent), var(--accent-2));\n      /* Delay phases the first cycle so a static capture lands mid-overshoot\n         rather than on the return leg, where every lane looks identical. */\n      animation: run 1.5s var(--ease) 1.1s infinite;\n    }\n    .s1 { --ease: var(--ease-out); }\n    .s2 { --ease: var(--spring-gentle); }\n    .s3 { --ease: var(--spring-stiff); }\n    .s4 { --ease: var(--spring-bouncy); }\n\n    /* Per-segment timing: the spring drives the outbound leg only. A reversed\n       spring would overshoot backwards past its own origin, which no spring does.\n       The 0% frame deliberately declares NO timing function so the segment\n       inherits each lane's own --ease; var() does not resolve for\n       animation-timing-function inside a keyframe, it silently falls back and\n       every lane ends up moving identically. */\n    @keyframes run {\n      0%   { left: 0; }\n      55%  { left: var(--travel); animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1); }\n      82%  { left: var(--travel); animation-timing-function: cubic-bezier(.65,0,.35,1); }\n      100% { left: 0; }\n    }\n    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\n      .dot { animation: none; left: var(--travel); }\n    }\n  </style>\n</head>\n<body>\n  <p class=\"head\"><b>Springs overshoot; cubic-beziers cannot.</b> <i>Dashed line = target.</i></p>\n  <div class=\"lane\"><b>ease-out</b><div class=\"track\"><div class=\"dot s1\"></div></div></div>\n  <div class=\"lane\"><b>--spring-gentle</b><div class=\"track\"><div class=\"dot s2\"></div></div></div>\n  <div class=\"lane\"><b>--spring-stiff</b><div class=\"track\"><div class=\"dot s3\"></div></div></div>\n  <div class=\"lane\"><b>--spring-bouncy</b><div class=\"track\"><div class=\"dot s4\"></div></div></div>\n</body>\n</html>\n"
}
